<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  launchd agent for the social publisher (Mac mini). Deliberately the SAME
  mechanism as com.marathon.shopifyreconcile: a user LaunchAgent in
  ~/Library/LaunchAgents running node against the repo checkout, with its logs
  under the repo. No second scheduling system is introduced.

  Differences from the reconciler, and why:

    · StartCalendarInterval instead of StartInterval. The reconciler is a
      2-minute poll because Publish is a thing Junid presses and then waits
      for. This is a CADENCE — one post and one story a day — and the times below ARE
      that cadence: a feed post every day at 11:00.

      There is deliberately NO second daily fire. A 16:30 story slot was added
      and then removed the same day: nothing publishes a story — the publisher
      has no story path at all — and the 16:30 fire therefore ran the ordinary
      publisher, which would have posted any approved-and-overdue item to the
      FEED at a time meant for stories. A slot that does the wrong thing
      quietly is worse than no slot. It comes back when stories are built.

      These entries must match SLOT_DAYS / SLOT_HOUR_SAST / STORY_HOUR_SAST in
      src/components/social/socialCore.js, which is what the queue shows Junid
      and what the generator writes into scheduledAt. If they drift, approved
      posts sit "due" until the next agent fire — late, never lost, but late.
      launchd Weekday numbering matches JS getUTCDay: 0/7 Sunday, 1 Monday.

    · THESE TIMES ARE THE HOST'S LOCAL TIME, NOT SAST. launchd has no timezone
      field and cannot be given one; StartCalendarInterval is evaluated against
      whatever the machine's clock says. The Mac mini is set to SAST (verified
      2026-08-24: `SAST +0200`), so 11:00 here IS 11:00 SAST — but that is a
      property of the machine, not of this file.

      Change the mini's timezone and every fire moves silently with it: no
      error, no log line, posts simply going out at the wrong hour. The
      installer therefore CHECKS the host timezone and refuses to install
      against a non-SAST clock rather than letting the two drift apart
      unnoticed. If this agent is ever wanted on a machine in another zone,
      convert the hours by hand — do not assume the label.

    · RunAtLoad is FALSE, unlike the reconciler. Loading the agent — after a
      reboot, or while installing it — must not fire a post. Nothing here is
      urgent enough to justify a publish at an unscheduled moment, and a
      surprise post to a live Instagram account is not recoverable.

    · A missed fire (the mini was asleep or off at the scheduled time) is run by launchd as
      soon as it wakes. That is the behaviour we want: an approved post goes
      out late rather than being skipped for the week.

  NODE PATH: /opt/homebrew/bin/node, the SAME interpreter the reconciler's
  agent names. This is an Apple-silicon Mac mini, where Homebrew installs under
  /opt/homebrew; /usr/local/bin/node — the Intel-era path this file used to
  name — DOES NOT EXIST on that machine. launchd resolves ProgramArguments[0]
  itself and does not consult PATH, so the EnvironmentVariables PATH below
  could not have rescued it: every fire would have died before node started,
  leaving nothing but a spawn error in social-launchd.err.log while the job
  still reported itself loaded. The installer rewrites this line to whichever
  node it actually finds and refuses to install if that binary is missing, so
  the two can never drift apart again.

  INSTALL: see scripts/social/SOCIAL-SETUP.md. Paths below assume the repo is
  checked out at /Users/marathonclub/marathon-store-app — change BOTH the
  ProgramArguments path and WorkingDirectory if it lives elsewhere.

  CREDENTIALS: nothing secret belongs in this file (it is committed to the
  repo). The platform tokens live in Google Secret Manager and are read at run
  time by the service account GOOGLE_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS points at. Only
  PATHS appear here, never values.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>Label</key><string>com.marathon.socialpublish</string>
  <key>ProgramArguments</key>
  <array>
    <string>/opt/homebrew/bin/node</string>
    <string>/Users/marathonclub/marathon-store-app/scripts/social/publish-runner.mjs</string>
  </array>
  <key>WorkingDirectory</key><string>/Users/marathonclub/marathon-store-app</string>
  <key>EnvironmentVariables</key>
  <dict>
    <key>PATH</key><string>/usr/local/bin:/opt/homebrew/bin:/usr/bin:/bin</string>
    <key>GOOGLE_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS</key><string>/Users/marathonclub/.config/marathon/shopify-reconciler-sa.json</string>
  </dict>
  <!-- ── A TICK, NOT A DAILY FIRE ─────────────────────────────────────────
       Every two minutes, the same shape as com.marathon.shopifyreconcile.

       The CADENCE has not moved: the generator still assigns 11:00 slots and
       the queue still shows them. What changed is where "when" lives. It is
       now entirely in each post's scheduledAt, and this agent simply asks the
       publisher what is DUE. A tick with nothing due exits in under a second
       having made zero platform calls.

       Two things this buys that a single daily fire could not:

         · POST NOW. The queue's button moves an approved post's scheduledAt to
           now; it goes out on the next tick instead of tomorrow. Nothing about
           publishing moved into the browser — the Meta credentials stay on
           this machine and this remains the only thing that has ever posted.

         · A missed fire stops mattering. Asleep at 11:00, or a post approved
           at 14:00 whose slot has passed, is picked up minutes later rather
           than waiting a day.

       Single-flight is the runner's lockfile, and double-posting is prevented
       by the claim transaction in publish.mjs — both already relied on by the
       daily fire, neither weakened by ticking more often. -->
  <key>StartInterval</key><integer>120</integer>
  <key>RunAtLoad</key><false/>
  <!-- launchd's own capture is the LAST-RESORT log: anything the runner could
       not handle itself (node failing to start, a syntax error) lands here.
       The readable per-run log is logs/social-publish.log, written and rotated
       by the runner. -->
  <key>StandardOutPath</key><string>/Users/marathonclub/marathon-store-app/logs/social-launchd.out.log</string>
  <key>StandardErrorPath</key><string>/Users/marathonclub/marathon-store-app/logs/social-launchd.err.log</string>
</dict>
</plist>
